#!/usr/bin/env python3
# weizigo agent runner — guarded execution wrapper for zig/agent builds.
#
#   tools/runner [options] [--] <command> [args...]
#   tools/runner --sweep                   (standalone sweep, no command)
#
# Wraps a command in an RSS guard + wall/CPU ceiling + zig optimization
# discipline.  The minimum stand on the 2026-07-29 host panic (see
# docs/infra/host/incident-2026-07-29.md).  The pattern is Opus 5's from
# the EXP-9 close-out: ReleaseFast by default, SIGKILL the process group
# at any ceiling breach, clear reporting on stderr, exit 124.
#
# Four guards, three default-on:
#   --rss-cap-mb 12288  SECONDARY bound: SIGKILL the process group when any
#                       member exceeds this RSS.  Generous now that the
#                       host guard exists — catches a single runaway
#                       allocation, not a legitimate 4.1 GB check.
#   --host-mem-floor-mb FLEET-AWARE guard (T362): read system-wide available
#                       memory; below a danger floor (default derived from
#                       hw.memsize, total // 8) SIGKILL the LARGEST member
#                       of the process group — a fleet citizen relieving
#                       host pressure before the host panics.  The per-
#                       process cap bounds ONE worker; five such workers
#                       would commit 40 GB.  The 2026-07-29 panic had
#                       system free ~46 MB of 48 GB before watchdogd
#                       starved — do not raise the floor casually.
#                       --no-host-guard disables.  WEIZIGO_HOST_MEM_AVAIL_MB
#                       injects the reading for the test (never exhaust
#                       the host to test the floor).
#   --max-wall 1800     (fallback for silent children) SIGKILL after this
#                       many wall seconds if no [progress] lines seen.
#                       macOS does not have timeout(1), so this is the
#                       project's only reliable wall-time backstop.
#   --max-cpu 3600      (fallback for silent children) SIGKILL when the
#                       sum of cumulative CPU seconds across ALL
#                       descendants exceeds this.  Reported per-PID on exit.
#
# Why multiple guards?  The 2026-07-29 panic proved that a single guard
# (per-process RSS) is not enough — and per-process RSS says nothing
# about the fleet.  A `zig test` binary spinning at 0% RSS sat undetected
# for 5 hours because name-based greps missed the anonymous process.  The
# host guard catches fleet-wide memory pressure; the wall/CPU ceilings
# catch the silent long/throughput cases; the progress watchdog catches
# stuck children that still emit progress lines.
#
# macOS timeout(1) gap.  macOS ships no `timeout` utility (it is
# Linux-only / coreutils-only).  Agents reaching for `timeout` to bound
# a run silently get no bound.  This runner replaces that missing tool
# for the project — documented in docs/infra/host/macos-timeout-gap.md.
#
# --sweep mode.  Standalone: lists the top CPU consumers on the host,
# sorted by cumulative CPU time, to help find orphaned zig processes
# (which compile to anonymous .zig-cache names).  No command needed.
#
# WORKER-CHANNEL (2026-07-29): --task-id for identity, heartbeat emission
# to untracked/heartbeat.jsonl, directive checking via tasks.json.  On every
# invocation: prints pending directives on stderr, exits non-zero when a
# `pause` or `kill` directive is pending.  Heartbeat on every exit (success,
# failure, or guard kill) so liveness shows real runs even if ungraceful.
#
# T370 (2026-08-06): identity also propagates via MANAGENT_TASK_ID from the
# dispatch wrappers (bin/subagent, bin/ollama-subagent, tools/bakeoff.sh) so
# nested invocations inherit it.  A run with neither --task-id nor the env
# var is a DEGRADED mode and warns loudly: heartbeats land under
# runner/<pid>, liveness cannot attribute them, and directives cannot reach
# the run.  Directives are re-checked every --directive-poll-s during the
# run, so a `managent tell <id> pause|kill` lands in a RUNNING command too,
# not just at launch.
#
# T214 (2026-08-01): progress watchdog replaces wall/CPU ceilings.
# The RSS cap is the one safety-critical guard (memory exhaustion panics the
# host).  Wall and CPU ceilings are bad stuck-detectors — they kill correct
# long-running jobs.
#
# Two tiers:
#   1. RSS cap (--rss-cap-mb 4096) — always on, hard kill.  No progress earns
#      an exemption from a memory-exhaustion kill.
#   2. Progress watchdog (--progress-timeout 600) — kills when the child emits
#      no [progress] line for N seconds.  A child that reports every few
#      minutes runs as long as it needs.  Fallback: when no progress lines are
#      ever seen, the traditional --max-cpu / --max-wall ceilings apply.
#
# All flags are pass-through except the guard itself.  Exit code 124
# (timeout convention) on guard kill, or the wrapped command's exit code.
#
# Standard library only.  No external dependencies.

